      {
        "review_id": 542928209,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r542928209-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Dash of culture",
        "text": "The Pashupatinath Temple is perhaps one of the most visited attractions in all of Nepal and especially significant to Hindus because of its deity, Lord Pashupatinath. This temple complex is on UNESCO World Heritage Sites's list Since 1979. Hence, we were mentally prepared for extremely long queues since many people had experienced this in the past.\nHowever, to our good luck, since we went here late in the evening around 7pm, there were barely a few people milling around in the main temple and we got to go straight to the main altar and view it from the outside while taking our time to spend a few minutes in silence and prayer. We then also visited some of the smaller temples right beside it and these seem to be steeped in legend and history.\nThe most impressive aspect of this temple was the massive Nandi made from bronze along with the dozens of lamps, sculptures and idols that complete the visual palette along similar lines. We did not get a chance to explore the surrounding temples, ashrams, forest sections and pyre sites along the banks of the sacred Bagmati river but the visit to the main temple proved to be unique in energy and visual appeal.\nWe also appreciated the service offered at the entry gates for all tourists who come here to have their footwear stored in bags and returned to them after their visit for no charge at all. The lane leading up to the main temple is thronged by vendors and shops selling all the usual paraphernalia and artifacts belonging to the Hindu deity.",
